<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Repo-wide default test filter (docs/sonnet-briefs/brief-test-default-fast.md). The exclusion is a
  property of the repository, not of the command someone types: plain `dotnet test`, `dotnet test
  tests/Ui.Tests`, an IDE test run, and CI all inherit this via Directory.Build.props's
  RunSettingsFilePath, with nothing to remember and nothing to get wrong.

  Category=Benchmark marks anything that should never run in a routine test pass, the 500k-shape
  TIMED sweeps this project's own history shows are the reason "dotnet test" got slow. Opt in with
  circuitrf.benchmark.runsettings (see that file), not a command-line filter argument: this SDK's
  VSTest version ANDs a command-line filter against this file's filter rather than overriding it, so
  filtering for Category=Benchmark alone would resolve to an impossible AND and match nothing.
  Verified directly, not assumed.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TestCaseFilter>Category!=Benchmark</TestCaseFilter>
  </RunConfiguration>

  <!--
    Every run leaves a machine-readable record of WHICH tests failed, at a fixed path
    (tests/<Project>/TestResults/last-run.trx), overwritten each run.

    This exists because "which tests failed?" was repeatedly costing a SECOND full run to answer. The
    console logger does print failures, but a long run's output is routinely piped through a grep, or
    scrolled past, or truncated by whatever captured it — and a 1h25m benchmark tier that reports only
    "Failed: 5" with no names is an hour and a half thrown away. A .trx is written by the test host
    itself, so no amount of downstream filtering can lose it.

    Cost is negligible: one XML file written once at the end of the run (a few MB for the ~8,600-test
    Ui suite), no per-test overhead. TestResults/ is already gitignored.

    Read it with `tools/test-failures.sh` (no argument = the most recently written run).
  -->
  <LoggerRunSettings>
    <Loggers>
      <Logger friendlyName="trx">
        <Configuration>
          <LogFileName>last-run.trx</LogFileName>
        </Configuration>
      </Logger>
    </Loggers>
  </LoggerRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
